#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841a9b67-0c96-5f53-bea1-8160ed0f03f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275311} {:answer/id #uuid "d96bfb3c-421e-5de6-abb3-2ede78197b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275312} {:answer/id #uuid "4fff97d8-690b-5ee3-ad1c-5fbcb603bfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275313} {:answer/id #uuid "68f7b0ef-9692-524e-b6a1-eac62613dbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275314} {:answer/id #uuid "7d86c78a-bf63-5297-8757-4287e538900a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96bfb3c-421e-5de6-abb3-2ede78197b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275312} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c44a43-bfe1-55b9-83cf-27bb482f8148" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0a9853b-5f08-5935-9f58-b1a4704f7537" :question/math-academy-id "q-112874" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{5}-7{x}^{2}+2x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }10}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{-6}{5}$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(-6)$ is not a factor of $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db0cb1c-ddd2-5d21-93a2-77d908629596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275318} {:answer/id #uuid "ed59bdbb-62c4-542b-8280-0eb3578c2cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275319} {:answer/id #uuid "45db3c94-38e5-5ad3-a37c-7f0e88992089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275320} {:answer/id #uuid "00900e9b-3910-5b72-9916-acb816889f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275321} {:answer/id #uuid "b9bc64bd-bfeb-5d48-a031-fe0aa87cbfd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45db3c94-38e5-5ad3-a37c-7f0e88992089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275320} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfc2450-6b19-591a-9c9e-b5e95faf3ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22d2c056-6e08-5c31-9b27-7a4a15727ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-112875" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f24117-c57e-5e0f-80af-4799fc02e647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275325} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef500b3-9469-509a-9fd1-b05afbd76e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275326} {:answer/id #uuid "d06dbdbd-e5e3-5a84-93be-74fbdeed745f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275327} {:answer/id #uuid "128001bd-3ff3-5293-922d-80a141576af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275328} {:answer/id #uuid "7125f12d-70fc-5a1b-aadd-5877cb5712c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f24117-c57e-5e0f-80af-4799fc02e647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275325} :answer-field/id #uuid "0341e623-1c81-5888-8332-5f8b086b9f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf13e900-4dce-558a-80db-3ec5cf963787" :question/math-academy-id "q-112984" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x+4$ has a root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ is $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $-15$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =4(x+\\frac{1}{4})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{4},2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c47db36c-d5d4-5ebc-ba45-5f9e0e650fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332} {:answer/id #uuid "5a51eefa-f12c-5bce-ae4b-5b02d4838dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275333} {:answer/id #uuid "0984caa8-4320-5ed9-8fdb-1a3869a55695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275334} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1af6fc-5897-5a50-a2fa-425d697dbf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275335} {:answer/id #uuid "08edba16-cd8f-568b-a56b-be0a8ce5aa95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c47db36c-d5d4-5ebc-ba45-5f9e0e650fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f485854-2381-534f-af7c-b4632b31f533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1dcb895-1da8-588d-8542-43eae8980235" :question/math-academy-id "q-6669" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+27x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 3, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 9, & \\pm 18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea18803f-48cc-559b-977d-0d6cf60c5fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275339} {:answer/id #uuid "236c8a02-b896-543c-afdd-4f31b8cff76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275340} {:answer/id #uuid "d88aee8e-e78a-5122-a231-8c4b90098694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275341} {:answer/id #uuid "91d5b2ef-e22d-578c-9c9b-5f87dc0bc77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275342} {:answer/id #uuid "1e669f11-b7c3-5b46-bb9f-089630405392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea18803f-48cc-559b-977d-0d6cf60c5fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275339} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec5345e-a178-50b6-9b58-5aa8b0bece85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da17c4ed-4ab3-57b1-81d4-3a233a0bd886" :question/math-academy-id "q-83840" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{5}+21{x}^{3}+16x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7,$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm \\frac{7}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)"}]}
